C = [
("h1", "4. Romans 14:13–23: The Stumblingblock and the Work of God"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 14:13–23 turns from the judgment of a brother to the harm done to him. The strong believer may be right that nothing is unclean of itself, and Paul agrees with him, and yet Paul tells him not to use that liberty in a way that grieves or destroys a brother. The limit is owed to the brother, and its ground is that Christ died for him."),
("sub", "4.1 The turn: judge this rather"),
("p", "Paul changes the sense of his own word. “Let us not therefore judge one another any more: but judge this rather, that no man put a stumblingblock or an occasion to fall in his brother’s way” (Romans 14:13). The first judging is the condemnation of a brother over meats and days, and Paul forbids it. The second is a decision, taken about oneself, not to put something in a brother’s way. A “stumblingblock” is something over which a man falls, and an “occasion to fall” is a cause of his falling. Piper defines the first as Paul uses it here: “This is what it means to put a stumbling block in someone’s way: enticing them to do what their conscience condemns.” The volume agrees with the definition as a reading of verses 14 and 23, and it adds only that Paul’s own word for the harm is wider than the definition: he speaks of grief, of destroying, of offence, of being made weak (Romans 14:15, 20, 21)."),
("sub", "4.2 Paul’s own conviction"),
("p", "“I know, and am persuaded by the Lord Jesus, that there is nothing unclean of itself: but to him that esteemeth any thing to be unclean, to him it is unclean” (Romans 14:14). Paul says plainly where he stands. He is on the side of the strong in the matter of the food. He does not tell the weak man that he is right to count meat unclean. He tells the strong man that the weak man’s conscience makes the meat unclean to him. Constable states the same point from another letter: “God has not forbidden Christians from eating any food (1 Timothy 4:3-4),” and the passage he cites says that “every creature of God is good, and nothing to be refused, if it be received with thanksgiving” (1 Timothy 4:4). The volume holds that Paul’s settled view in these matters is the view of the strong, and that he asks the strong to give up the exercise of it for a brother’s sake."),
("sub", "4.3 Grief, charity and destruction"),
("p", "“But if thy brother be grieved with thy meat, now walkest thou not charitably. Destroy not him with thy meat, for whom Christ died” (Romans 14:15). The verse has two parts. The first is a test of conduct: if a brother is grieved by what you eat, you are not walking in love. The second is a prohibition: do not destroy him, and the reason is that Christ died for him. The word “destroy” appears again in verse 20: “For meat destroy not the work of God.” The volume reads the “work of God” as the brother, and does not say more about what the destruction is than the text says. Piper writes that “What is at stake is the destruction of the weaker brother, and maybe ourselves in the process.” Paul does not say in this chapter what the destruction is, and the volume does not add to it."),
("sub", "4.4 The kingdom is not meat and drink"),
("p", "“Let not then your good be evil spoken of: for the kingdom of God is not meat and drink; but righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Ghost” (Romans 14:16, 17). The sentence is read here in its setting. It says what the kingdom of God is not, meat and drink, and in what it consists, righteousness, peace and joy in the Holy Ghost. Its purpose in the chapter is to put meat and drink in their place. The volume does not use the verse to describe the Kingdom promised to Israel, which is read in Prophecy & Mystery Volume 3, and it does not use it to describe the Body’s calling. “For he that in these things serveth Christ is acceptable to God, and approved of men” (Romans 14:18). The verse is a conclusion about service, and the volume rests no other doctrine on it."),
("p", "“Let us therefore follow after the things which make for peace, and things wherewith one may edify another” (Romans 14:19). The word “edify” is the word Paul uses in 1 Corinthians 8:1, “charity edifieth,” and in 1 Corinthians 10:23, “all things edify not.” In Romans 14 it names the aim of the believer’s conduct toward a brother."),
("sub", "4.5 Flesh, wine and anything"),
("p", "“It is good neither to eat flesh, nor to drink wine, nor any thing whereby thy brother stumbleth, or is offended, or is made weak” (Romans 14:21). Paul names two examples and adds a third clause that covers the rest: “nor any thing whereby thy brother stumbleth.” The verse does not command abstinence from flesh or wine. It says what is good where a brother would stumble. The volume reports the verse as written. It builds no doctrine on wine or on any other drink, and it says nothing of any matter that Paul does not name, because the verse is about a brother who is weak and about the strong man’s conduct toward him, and the limit stands only where those two are present."),
("sub", "4.6 Faith, doubt and the last verse"),
("p", "“Hast thou faith? have it to thyself before God. Happy is he that condemneth not himself in that thing which he alloweth” (Romans 14:22). The strong man’s conviction is real and is kept “before God.” It is not paraded before the weak. Then the chapter ends: “And he that doubteth is damned if he eat, because he eateth not of faith: for whatsoever is not of faith is sin” (Romans 14:23). The verse is read in its setting, which is eating. The person is the one who doubts whether he may eat, and eats. The faith is his persuasion that the act is permitted, and the sin is acting against that persuasion. The KJV’s “damned” is the translators’ word in a sentence about a man who eats against his own doubt, and the volume reads it there, as a verdict on that act. It does not turn the verse into a general rule that every doubt is a sin or into a statement about the ground of salvation, which Paul states elsewhere in other terms. Piper’s definition of the stumblingblock, quoted above, ties it to eating against conscience, and the volume agrees that verse 23 is the reason the stumblingblock is a real harm: the weak man who follows the strong man’s example eats against his conscience."),
("ctbl", "Romans 14:13–23 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "He commands a decision not to put a stumblingblock; he does not command abstinence", "14:13"),
    ("What limit?", "Do not grieve or destroy a brother with meat; follow peace and edification", "14:15, 19, 20"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To the brother whose conscience is weak", "14:15, 21"),
    ("What ground?", "Christ died for him; meat is not the kingdom; the work of God", "14:15, 17, 20"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "What the destruction is; whether wine is wrong; how a weak conscience grows strong", "Chapters 4 and 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "4.7 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Paul agrees with the strong that nothing is unclean of itself, and he asks the strong to give up the exercise of that liberty where a brother would be harmed.",
    "The limit is owed to the brother, and its ground is that Christ died for him.",
    "Meat and drink are put in their place by the sentence that the kingdom of God is not meat and drink.",
    "Romans 14:23 is read in its eating setting and is not made a general rule of doubt.",
  ]),
]
